<script>
	import Callout from '$lib/components/Callout.svelte';
</script>

Hard-coded test data goes stale and collides. Curlew ships a catalogue of dynamic
functions — UUIDs, timestamps, and a full **faker** suite — that you drop straight
into request bodies with `{{$...}}`. Pass `--seed` and the same "random" values come
back every run, so failures are reproducible. Fields that look like secrets are
redacted everywhere automatically.

## The collection

This one posts to `httpbin.org/anything`, which echoes your payload back — so you can
run it yourself and see exactly what Curlew sent.

```yaml
# collections/signup.yaml
name: Signup payload
requests:
  - name: Generate a signup
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "https://httpbin.org/anything"
      body:
        id: "{{$uuid}}"
        name: "{{$faker.fullName}}"
        email: "{{$faker.email}}"
        phone: "{{$faker.phone}}"
        city: "{{$faker.city}}"
        ssn: "{{$faker.ssn}}"          # auto-sensitive → [REDACTED] in every output
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        "$.json.id":
          matches: "^[0-9a-f-]{36}$"
        "$.json.name":
          exists: true
        "$.json.email":
          matches: "@example\\.com$"
```

## Run it

```bash
curlew run collections/signup.yaml --seed 42
```

```
Collection: Signup payload
  ✓ Generate a signup  200  1972ms

────────────────────────────────
  1 request(s): 1 passed, 0 failed (1973ms)
```

<Callout type="tip">
	Drop the <code>--seed</code> and every value is fresh per run; keep it and the run
	is byte-for-byte reproducible. <code>{'{{$faker.ssn}}'}</code> is recognised as
	sensitive, so even though it's sent to the server it shows as
	<code>[REDACTED]</code> in terminal, JSON, and log output.
</Callout>

## Beyond the basics

The catalogue is large — `{{$faker.firstName}}`, `{{$faker.address}}`,
`{{$faker.company}}`, `{{$randomInt}}`, `{{$timestamp}}`, `{{$randomPassword('16')}}`
and many more — and every value participates in the same redaction machinery as any
other variable. See the [Faker & redaction examples](/examples#feature=faker) for the
full set in action.
